WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:06.080
You know what I hate? I mean, really hate?&nbsp;
Passwords. There's too many of them. They're hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:11.480
to keep up with. I'm losing them all the time. I'm&nbsp;
going to go out on a limb, probably not very far,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:16.280
and suggest that you probably don't like them&nbsp;
either. Well, what if I told you there was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:23.080
way that we could get rid of these things without&nbsp;
compromising security? In fact, a way that allows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:28.680
us to improve usability of the system while&nbsp;
also improving security, which is something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:33.520
that's hard to do, both of these at the same time.&nbsp;
That would be interesting, right? What's the name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:43.000
of the technology that lets us do this? Well, it's&nbsp;
called Fast Identity Online or FiDO for short. No,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:48.360
not that Fido. Although he really hates&nbsp;
passwords, too. And what does FIDO do? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:52.880
it's a protocol that allows us to get rid of&nbsp;
passwords by replacing them with something we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:58.080
call passkeys. And it's not something brand new,&nbsp;
even though you may not have heard of it before.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:02.960
It's been around since 2013. The FIDO Alliance,&nbsp;
this industry consortium that put together the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:09.680
standard. And in fact, there are more than 250&nbsp;
organizations that are using the FIDO standard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:17.000
and are part of this alliance. And now what's new,&nbsp;
is there's a newer version of this called FIDO 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:21.320
which incorporates two new elements that&nbsp;
are particularly important. Hardware-based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:27.920
authentication, so now I can use biometrics or&nbsp;
hardware-based tokens. Think a phone. That is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:33.840
something I have. And then using my face to unlock&nbsp;
the phone, something that I am--that we can do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:40.560
that sort of thing. And we have support now for&nbsp;
web browsers, which now extend the use cases and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:47.080
the possibility of all the different places where&nbsp;
we can use FIDO. Okay, so how does all this magic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:52.520
work? Well, let's start with a quick review of&nbsp;
cryptography. Some of these concepts are going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:57.920
to be important in understanding how this could be&nbsp;
possible. First of all, we have two main different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:05.600
classes of cryptography algorithms. We have&nbsp;
symmetric and we have asymmetric. With a symmetric&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:12.400
algorithm, I have a single key, a symmetric&nbsp;
key. And that key is used to encrypt as well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:18.720
as decrypt. So if I encrypt a message, I use this&nbsp;
key. I want to decrypt it, I use the very same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:24.640
key. So that means both sides have to have that&nbsp;
key and know what it is. That's how that works,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:30.280
symmetric, because it's the same key on both&nbsp;
sides. However, when we go over here to asymmetric&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:36.360
cryptography, it's a different situation. We have&nbsp;
to mathematically related keys that share this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:43.920
unique property that whatever I encrypt with one&nbsp;
can only in fact be decrypted with the other and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:52.160
vice versa. If I encrypt with this, then I can&nbsp;
decrypt only with that. So these are related,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:58.440
but they are not equal. Now we're going to use&nbsp;
that special quality in order to do the magic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:05.240
that we're going to do with these passkeys. For&nbsp;
instance, and in fact what we do, we refer to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:13.680
these one of them as a public key and the other&nbsp;
as a private key. And so let's take an example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:20.840
of how we would use these. So here is our user&nbsp;
going to log into this website. First of all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:27.640
they need to register. How does the registration&nbsp;
flow work? Well, it's sort of like this user is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:34.200
going to send some registration information over&nbsp;
to the web server. And as they're doing that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:42.040
in this process of registration, the user on their&nbsp;
device is going to generate both a public key and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:48.840
a private key. So an example of each of these. &nbsp;
The private key is going to stay on this device&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:55.480
and never leave it. And we're going to lock it&nbsp;
down with some sort of biometric or other strong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.480 --> 00:04:00.760
authentication capability. So the private key&nbsp;
stays on the device. The public key, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:06.840
we share with the web server. The web server&nbsp;
takes that public key and puts it in a database&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:13.880
and associate it with this user. Now, that's the&nbsp;
registration piece. Now, what do we do next is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.880 --> 00:04:19.200
let's say this user, we do that one time. Now&nbsp;
the user wants to log into the system. So now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:25.000
what we need to be able to do is authenticate.&nbsp;
The authentication flow is going to work like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:30.800
this. So the user is going to send information&nbsp;
to the web server. For instance, they're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:37.400
to put it in their username and say, "Hey, this&nbsp;
is me. I'd like to log in." The web server says,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:43.760
"Oh yeah, I remember you because you registered&nbsp;
with me before. I'm going to pull in your public&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:48.120
key because you shared that with me." And by the&nbsp;
way, there's never a problem with sharing a public&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:53.720
key. That's why we call it a public key. Only&nbsp;
problem is if you share a private key. So we take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:59.920
the public key that's been shared in advance,&nbsp;
and we're going to then calculate a challenge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:05.440
of some sort. So that is a specially designed&nbsp;
message that's going to have some information,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:10.280
probably a timestamp and other things like that&nbsp;
that are unique. And we're going to encrypt it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:17.880
with that user's public key. And remember, if&nbsp;
it's been encrypted with the user's public key,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:22.200
only they can decrypt it because they're the only&nbsp;
ones that have the private key and they've locked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:27.560
it down on their device. That's this is where we&nbsp;
start getting the real security capabilities. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:34.240
sends the challenge over to the user. The user&nbsp;
decrypts it with their private key. And then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:38.960
if they're able to read it, they're able to see&nbsp;
what's the challenge message. And they can then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:46.480
respond to the web server encrypting the response&nbsp;
that proves that they have read the challenge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:51.560
message and they will encrypt that again with&nbsp;
their private key and send that back to the web&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:59.520
server. The web server then is going to take that&nbsp;
and we do the final step. That is verification.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:04.640
Verification means the web server now is going&nbsp;
to take this response message that we got. We're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:10.840
going to decrypt it with the public key, and we're&nbsp;
going to compare it and see if it works. If the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:17.880
challenge we sent matches the message we got back,&nbsp;
then we can prove that this user is in fact who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:23.920
they claim to be and we will allow them to log&nbsp;
in. Notice what was not ever happening in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:30.000
case. There were no passwords. At no time did you&nbsp;
see a password come out of my hands. In this case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:35.400
we stored the private key on the device. Always we&nbsp;
keep the public key, share it with the web server.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:40.880
The web server associates that with the user,&nbsp;
and then we just do a series of challenges. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:46.840
for this user, all they have to do in order when&nbsp;
this challenge comes across, unlock their device,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:52.360
indicate maybe on the device that this is the&nbsp;
website that I want to log into, approve that, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.360 --> 00:06:59.720
the rest of it happens automagically. Okay, let's&nbsp;
review really quickly what we've talked about.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:07.080
So with FIDO, we keep the private key on the&nbsp;
device itself and we guard it with multi-factor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:13.240
authentication. So that way nobody else can see&nbsp;
it. It remains private and therefore only that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.240 --> 00:07:20.080
user can decrypt and encrypt with that key. The&nbsp;
public key, however, is shared. That's why we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:24.880
call it public. We share that with the service&nbsp;
provider, with the website or what have you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:29.840
And that's okay. Because of the mathematical&nbsp;
relationship between these two, having one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:35.680
doesn't allow you to figure out what the other is,&nbsp;
which is nice. Now, one of the things that really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:40.080
nice about this, not only do I get to get rid of&nbsp;
passwords, but there's some downstream effects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:47.400
that occur from that. For instance, it resists&nbsp;
phishing attacks. So the phishing issue often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:53.360
occurs because someone is able to coax you out of&nbsp;
your credentials. You click on a website and log&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.360 --> 00:08:00.080
into a bogus website and you give the attacker&nbsp;
your login your user ID and password. If there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:05.280
no password, in other words, only passkeys&nbsp;
in the form of these challenges and crypto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:11.880
mechanisms, then there really is no password for&nbsp;
the phisher to gather in the first place. So we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:18.080
resist a lot of those phishing attacks. That's&nbsp;
a nice side effect. Another thing is it resists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:25.680
replay attacks. So a replay attack is one where&nbsp;
a user or an attacker might sit on a network and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:30.240
see the information that you're sending when you&nbsp;
go to send your password over the network. Maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:35.120
it's in an encrypted form in the form of a hash,&nbsp;
and they take that hash and they don't even have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:39.520
to know what it says. They just need a copy of&nbsp;
it. And then they replay it and send it to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:45.680
web server as if they were you. In this case,&nbsp;
we've got a system of challenges. They wouldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:50.600
know what to send because they'd have to see what&nbsp;
was coming in the first place. So we resist replay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:57.320
attacks. And finally it gets rid of passwords&nbsp;
for us. Those things that we hate, the things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.320 --> 00:09:03.240
that are hard to keep up with, the things that can&nbsp;
be forgotten, the things that people write down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:07.680
the things that people are not very creative at&nbsp;
coming up with in the first place. In this kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:13.960
of a situation, there's no passwords involved. The&nbsp;
passkeys are all based upon information with these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.960 --> 00:09:19.080
public and private keys, which are automatically&nbsp;
generated, and they're complex and strong and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.080 --> 00:09:24.160
user doesn't have to keep up with it. And you know&nbsp;
what? This has been around for a while. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.160 --> 00:09:31.920
IBM has been supporting FIDO 2 since 2018, and&nbsp;
there's a lot of other vendors that are jumping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:36.840
on board as well as I told you to begin with.&nbsp;
So this is something that I believe is going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:43.560
to be the future of logins and authentication.&nbsp;
And the beautiful thing is the future is free&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.560 --> 00:09:49.840
of passwords. Thanks for watching. If you found&nbsp;
this video interesting and would like to learn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.840 --> 00:09:56.960
more about cybersecurity, please remember&nbsp;
to hit like and subscribe to this channel.

